# G124 — Image Browser

*Pilot — real Innate syntax, v2. Prose insights from `image-browser.md`
still apply. This is the `where`-clause showcase: what I modeled as a
`FilterPredicate` ADT collapses into native filter constraints.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Image Explorer

[image-browser @images
    IN ORDER TO
        let the user walk through a filtered, sorted set of images
        with a 2D grid cursor, keeping recently-viewed thumbnails warm
        via an LRU cache

    @grid-cols: 4
    @thumbnail-capacity: 100

    @visible <- @images
        where
            name contains ".jpg" or name contains ".png"
            and tag has @current-tag
            and width at least 1024
        ordered by created descending
        limited to what fits the 4-column grid at current scroll

    sync @ui-events {
        left  -> [grid[move {cursor: @cursor, direction: "left"}]]
        right -> [grid[move {cursor: @cursor, direction: "right"}]]
        up    -> [grid[move {cursor: @cursor, direction: "up"}]]
        down  -> [grid[move {cursor: @cursor, direction: "down"}]]
        home  -> [grid[home {cursor: @cursor}]]
        end   -> [grid[end  {cursor: @cursor}]]
        enter -> [viewer[open {image: @selected}]]
    }

    for @entry in @visible
        @thumb <- ![lru {cache: @thumbnails, key: @entry:id}]
                  || [thumb[generate {entry: @entry, size: 128}]]
        #tile
            row: @entry:grid-row
            col: @entry:grid-col
            data: @thumb
            highlighted: @entry:id == @selected:id

    @selected <- @visible at-index @cursor:flat-index
]

---

## Verb Surface (resolver contract)

Split across three namespaces because the browser, grid cursor, and
LRU cache are independently reusable resolvers:

[image
    list       {}                              -> [@image]
    tags       {image}                          -> [@tag]
    thumbnail  {image, size}                   -> @blob
]

[grid
    new        {cols, total}                   -> @cursor
    move       {cursor, direction}             -> @cursor
    home       {cursor}                        -> @cursor
    end        {cursor}                        -> @cursor
    row-col    {cursor}                        -> @(row, col)
    flat-index {cursor}                        -> @index
]

[lru
    new        {capacity}                       -> @cache
    get        {cache, key}                     -> @value-or-nil
    put        {cache, key, value}              -> @evicted-key-or-nil
    contains   {cache, key}                     -> @bool
]

---

## The `where` clause replaces the Query ADT

In the v1 spec, I modeled the query language as a struct:

```
Query { filters: [FilterPredicate], sort_by: SortBy, order: Order }
with FilterPredicate :: NameContains(s) | TagHas(s) | SizeOver(n) | ...
```

In real Innate this dissolves into prose the resolver parses:

[image-browser[list @all
    where
        name contains ".jpg"
        and tag has "nature"
        and size under 5 megabytes
        and created after [[2026-01-01]]
    ordered by size ascending
]

The author writes what they mean. The resolver extracts constraints
into whatever internal representation it uses. The ADT is now an
implementation detail of the `image` resolver, not a user-facing vocabulary.

Filter composition is AND-of-predicates — matching the v1 insight —
but the author never assembles a `Vec<FilterPredicate>`. They write
`and` between clauses.

---

## Cursor navigation and LRU both benefit from native Innate idioms

**Grid move** as a verb rather than an enum argument:

[grid[move {cursor: @c, direction: "down"}]]

is directional; the resolver handles partial-last-row clamping
internally. The `Down` variant of a `GridMove` enum becomes a
string literal.

**LRU cache access** uses fulfillment:

@thumb <- ![lru {cache: @thumbs, key: @entry:id}]
          || [thumb[generate {entry: @entry, size: 128}]]

Reads as: "try to find the cached thumbnail; if the cache doesn't
have it, generate it." Cache miss becomes a natural side-effect path;
cache hit returns without invoking the producer. This is the same
memoization pattern from v1 but expressed in Innate's idiom of
`![search] || @fallback` rather than an explicit `(value, miss-bool)`
tuple return.

---

## Breadcrumb navigation is a stack, but Innate handles paths natively

[image-browser @images
    IN ORDER TO walk through directory subtrees

    at @user-entered-subdir @name
        [path[push @name]]

    at @user-pressed-up
        [path[pop]]

    #current-path -> @path:current   ;; e.g. "/photos/2026"
]

The stack is held by the `path` resolver. The path string is derived
via `@path:current`, not stored as a field. This matches the v1 insight
that "path is a fold" — but the fold lives in the resolver.

---

## Demo

[demo
    IN ORDER TO exercise filter, sort, navigation, and LRU caching

    @images <- [image[list]]
    @filtered <- @images
        where
            name contains ".jpg"
            and tag has "nature"
        ordered by size ascending

    -> @filtered count
    ;; -> 2      (alpha.jpg, delta.jpg from the v1 5-image demo)

    @c <- [grid[new {cols: 3, total: @filtered count}]]
    [grid[move {cursor: @c, direction: "right"}]]
    -> (@filtered at-index @c:flat-index):name
    ;; -> "delta.jpg"

    @cache <- [lru[new {capacity: 2}]]
    [lru[put {cache: @cache, key: 1, value: "a"}]]
    [lru[put {cache: @cache, key: 2, value: "b"}]]
    [lru[get {cache: @cache, key: 1}]]         ;; refreshes 1 to MRU
    [lru[put {cache: @cache, key: 3, value: "c"}]]
    -> @evicted                                 ;; -> 2 (was LRU)
]
